{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>But.</b> An expression of affirming the truth of the matter — as in ״אֲבָל אֲשֵׁמִים אֲנָחְנוּ״ (Genesis 42), ״אֲבָל בֵּן אֵין לָהּ״ (II Kings 4)." "<b>And you shall call his name Yitzchak.</b> After the laughter. And its numerical value — יֵצְחָק = 208 — corresponds to the ten trials, and the צ' = 90 years of Sarah, and the ח' = 8 days of circumcision, and the ק' = 100 years of Abraham." "<b>And I will establish My covenant with him.</b> Why is this stated? It is already written ״אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמֹר אַתָּה וְזַרְעֲךָ״? Rather, since it says ״וַהֲקִמֹתִי״ etc., one might think the children of Yishmael and the children of Keturah are included in the establishment — therefore it says ״וַהֲקִמֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי אִתּוֹ״ — and not with others.\n<b>And as for My covenant, which I will establish with Yitzchak.</b> Why is this stated? Rather, it teaches that he was holy from the womb. Alternatively, Rabbi Abba said: from here we learn an a fortiori argument regarding the son of the mistress over the son of the maidservant; it is written ״הִנֵּה בֵּרַכְתִּיו וְהִפְרֵיתִיו וְהִרְבֵּיתִיו״ — this is Yishmael — and a fortiori ״וְאֶת בְּרִיתִי אָקִים אֶת יִצְחָק״." "<b>And My covenant.</b> The covenant of circumcision shall be handed over to the seed of Yitzchak."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:10.281329665Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}